592186138387 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050051 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138387 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186138697 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138387 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186138752 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138387 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186476906 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138387 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186476950 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138387 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding Bounds When Rounding to a Whole Number" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138387 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186138164 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138387 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "61a5c363-0371-5820-a4e4-b21335406e50" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138388 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050934 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138388 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186138698 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138388 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186138753 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138388 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186476919 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138388 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186476925 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138388 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186536858 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138388 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding Bounds When Rounding to a Decimal Place" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138388 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186138165 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138388 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "e99cabd4-b713-50e1-83be-f8571b575803" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138389 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050415 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138389 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050436 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138389 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050792 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138389 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186138737 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138389 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186138801 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138389 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186476974 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138389 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186476975 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138389 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186538958 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138389 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186539011 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138389 #edb/ref 1039 "Bounding Areas and Volumes" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138389 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186138167 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138389 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "29217571-4597-5bbe-aecd-0d9a1d1aa1c1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138593 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186139294 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138593 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186139295 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138593 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186139296 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138593 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186139297 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138593 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186139298 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138593 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138593 #edb/ref 1045 "Degrees of Accuracy" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138593 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186139294 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138593 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186048438 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138593 #edb/ref 1050 420.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138593 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "88d9417f-4dcb-5b07-99a4-4fc3631a5617" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138698 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186135427 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138698 #edb/ref 1069 "q-154080" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138698 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138698 #edb/ref 1071 "The shoulder height of a moose $h$ measures $2.1\\,\\text{m}$ to the nearest tenth of a meter. Determine the upper and lower bounds of this measurement." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138698 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "66b93aa5-a873-551c-b039-7debf6d7af79" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138698 #edb/ref 1074 "Our measurement is given to the nearest $0.1\\,\\text{m}.$ So, the greatest possible error of this measurement is\n\n$$\n\\frac{0.1\\,\\text{m}}{2}=0.05\\,\\text{m}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the lower and upper bounds of this measurement as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{lower bound} & =2.1\\,\\text{m}-0.05\\,\\text{m} \\\\  & =2.05\\,\\text{m} \\\\ \\text{upper bound} & =2.1\\,\\text{m}+0.05\\,\\text{m} \\\\  & =2.15\\,\\text{m}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the exact value of $h$ lies between the lower and upper bound, we conclude that\n\n$$\n2.05\\,\\text{m}\\le h<2.15\\,\\text{m}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138737 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186135493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138737 #edb/ref 1069 "q-210524" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138737 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138737 #edb/ref 1071 "The dimensions of a rectangular floor are $12\\,\\text{m}\\times 3\\,\\text{m}$, where each dimension is measured to the nearest $1\\,\\text{m}.$ Rounded to the nearest square meter, what is the smallest possible area of the floor?\n\nTo the nearest square meter, the smallest possible area is\n{{field-1}}${\\text{m}}^{2}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138737 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a0cc887e-fde1-561f-a2a2-0e9220220d6b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138737 #edb/ref 1073 true #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138737 #edb/ref 1074 "Each dimension is given to the nearest $1\\,\\text{m}.$ So, the greatest possible error of these measurements is\n\n$$\n\\frac{1\\,\\text{m}}{2}=0.5\\,\\text{m}.\n$$\n\nWe wish to find the *smallest* possible area of the container. Therefore, we require the *lower bound* of each dimension.\n\nThe lower bounds for each dimension are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & 12\\,\\text{m}-0.5\\,\\text{m}=11.5\\,\\text{m} \\\\  & 3\\,\\text{m}-0.5\\,\\text{m}=2.5\\,\\text{m}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the smallest possible area of the container is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =11.5\\,\\text{m}\\times 2.5\\,\\text{m} \\\\  & =28.75\\,{\\text{m}}^{2} \\\\  & ≈29\\,{\\text{m}}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest square meter." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139294 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186139295 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139294 #edb/ref 1061 165169 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139294 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "cc0c262d-dd9e-52a1-a83b-9f1c0fe96262" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139294 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186139715 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139295 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186139296 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139295 #edb/ref 1061 165170 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139295 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "66ba1355-ab27-5d81-8157-a359b9801285" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139295 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186139716 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139296 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186139297 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139296 #edb/ref 1061 165171 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139296 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "bf5aa060-ae70-531a-8e1c-d165c1d25adb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139296 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186138387 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139297 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186139298 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139297 #edb/ref 1061 165174 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139297 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "7d898aa1-1cb4-5977-87b3-02ae7082146a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139297 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186138388 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139298 #edb/ref 1061 165177 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139298 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "761a41b0-39e8-54bb-9403-886bbe9ed521" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139298 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186138389 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139715 #edb/ref 1064 "We always introduce **errors** in our measurements whenever we round or approximate numbers. In this lesson, we will explore how to quantify and describe measurement errors.\n\nSuppose that a particular rope measures precisely $48.9\\,\\textrm{cm}.$ Now, imagine we take a meterstick and record a measurement of $50\\,\\textrm{cm}$ *to the nearest $10$ centimeters*. Let's mark this approximation on our meterstick.\n\n![](images/4e/4e5d5ca05f27aa612b3fdcc9430b495870ddf540630857b6227897d6b20c9e89.png)\n\nThe error of a measurement equals the difference between the rounded estimate and the \"true\" value. So, in this example, the error of our approximation equals\n\n$$\n50\\,\\textrm{cm} - 48.9\\,\\textrm{cm} = 1.1\\textrm{cm}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nWe often don't know the \"true\" measurement in practical problems. So, the next best thing is to determine a **lower bound** and an **upper bound** for our measurement:\n\n- The *lower* bound of a measurement is its *smallest* possible value.\n\n- The *upper* bound of a measurement is its *largest* possible value.\n\nLet's figure out the lower and upper bounds of our $50\\,\\textrm{cm}$ approximation:\n\n- The *lower bound* is $45 \\, \\textrm{cm}$ because this is the *smallest* value that gives $50 \\, \\textrm{cm}$ when rounded to the nearest $10\\,\\textrm{cm}.$\n\n![](images/da/da9cd6208cef81c6fe34fbba9b4271fa3ce1f30df69eb3c631ce6fea4f12e3a3.png)\n\n- The *upper bound* is $55 \\, \\textrm{cm}$ because this is (almost) the *largest* value that gives $50 \\, \\textrm{cm}$ when rounded to the nearest $10\\,\\textrm{cm}.$\n\n![](images/23/23a195d88326b587e1864ef04e7b05f74d675f1ddc987baa2cf586af599eb4a9.png)\n\nLet's use the letter $l$ to denote the \"true\" length of the rope. Then, we can combine our lower and upper bound measurements into a single statement, as follows:\n\n$$\n45\\,\\textrm{cm} \\leq l \\lt 55\\,\\textrm{cm}\n$$\n\nNotice that we used a *strict* \"<\" inequality for the upper bound. We cannot include $55\\textrm{cm}$ in our set of values because this number rounds to $60\\,\\textrm{cm},$ not $50\\,\\textrm{cm}$.\n\nWe can visualize the lower and upper bounds on our diagram as follows:\n\n![](images/1f/1fdacb0787e8316a78da1ad65aa177e9a6f29215fa0d53aef88b0eb33326218d.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139715 #edb/ref 1065 8751 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139715 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "d44fcde5-ad8a-596b-a6ac-955ff40b6bd9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139715 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139716 #edb/ref 1064 "So, we've seen how to calculate lower and upper bounds. Before we consider another example, note the following:\n\n- The lower and upper bound depend on our approximation's **degree of accuracy**.\n\n- In the last example, the degree of accuracy was $10\\,\\textrm{cm}$ because the rope was measured to the nearest $10$ centimeters.\n\n- If we change the degree of accuracy, the lower and upper bounds will also change. So, for example, if we measured the rope to the nearest $1\\,\\textrm{cm},$ the lower and upper bounds would be different.\n\n- The **greatest possible error** is the largest amount the \"true\" value can be from the approximation.\n\n- This greatest possible error is always one-half of the degree of accuracy. So, in the last example, the greatest possible error in our measurement was\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{10\\,\\textrm{cm}}{2} = {\\color{blue}{5\\,\\textrm{cm}}}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the greatest possible error to compute the lower and upper bounds quickly:\n\n- To compute the lower bound of an approximation, we *subtract* the greatest possible error from the approximation. So, for the previous example, we have\n\n$$\n\\textrm{lower bound} = 50\\,\\textrm{cm} - {\\color{blue}{5\\,\\textrm{cm}}} = 45\\,\\textrm{cm.}\n$$\n\n- To compute the upper bound of an approximation, we *add* the greatest possible error to the approximation. So, for the previous example, we have\n\n$$\n\\textrm{upper bound} = 50\\,\\textrm{cm} + {\\color{blue}{5\\,\\textrm{cm}}} = 55\\,\\textrm{cm.}\n$$\n\nLet's see another example." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139716 #edb/ref 1065 8752 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139716 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "f1808067-d542-577c-a2d3-085e0fb1df59" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139716 #edb/ref 1067 "The Greatest Possible Error" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186538226 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186539064 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186538226 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186539066 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186538226 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186539088 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186538226 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045464 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186538226 #edb/ref 1045 "Degrees of Accuracy" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186538226 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186539064 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186538226 #edb/ref 1048 #edb/ref 17592186046647 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186538226 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186048438 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186538226 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "b2fa5316-e98c-5047-902c-0d6c438a9d06" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186538282 #edb/ref 1080 "2.09\\,\\text{m}\\le h<2.11\\,\\text{m}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186538282 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186538282 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "16fac51a-9457-5261-b22b-f89d79af0f70" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186538308 #edb/ref 1080 "12.3\\,{\\text{m}}^{3}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186538308 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186538308 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "255bade8-0a89-5de2-8a24-9fd1e2100b17" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186538323 #edb/ref 1080 "29" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186538323 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186538323 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "2acee517-33dc-5f55-a0b3-f02e6c6263fc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186538329 #edb/ref 1080 "2.0\\,\\text{m}\\le h<2.2\\,\\text{m}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186538329 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186538329 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "2e710c8a-03cf-5964-8f07-e6abecb91318" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186538335 #edb/ref 1080 "12.7\\,{\\text{m}}^{3}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186538335 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186538335 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "31e67d7e-4e60-5f06-8489-9ee7a57c5092" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186538391 #edb/ref 1080 "2.095\\,\\text{m}\\le h<2.105\\,\\text{m}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186538391 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186538391 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "4c4403cc-a3af-50f8-91d2-7e006f9ca2bc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186538448 #edb/ref 1080 "2.05\\,\\text{m}\\le h<2.15\\,\\text{m}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186538448 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186538448 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "61ad7ba7-c6e6-5a77-88a2-8f331b4e6cc2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186538593 #edb/ref 1080 "14.2\\,{\\text{m}}^{3}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186538593 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186538593 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "995c6b46-9527-5d81-9c6e-1f7397ab9bcf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186538631 #edb/ref 1080 "13.0\\,{\\text{m}}^{3}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186538631 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186538631 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "a64f0aa8-5dbd-5dd3-ae18-7c76124f8c7b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186538659 #edb/ref 1080 "13.5\\,{\\text{m}}^{3}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186538659 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186538659 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "b6fd67f7-1c22-5e81-be9b-2698dfa44075" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186538742 #edb/ref 1080 "1.1\\,\\text{m}\\le h<3.1\\,\\text{m}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186538742 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186538742 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "de59b963-7146-502e-8106-8eeb734cdb88" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186538823 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186538308 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186538823 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186538335 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186538823 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186538593 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186538823 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186538631 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186538823 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186538659 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186538823 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186538823 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186538823 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186538631 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186538823 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "278391dd-df9b-5ab8-b856-03ca04c5de44" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186539011 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186538823 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186539011 #edb/ref 1069 "q-46386" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186539011 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186539011 #edb/ref 1071 "The dimensions of a cubic water tank are $2.4\\,\\text{m}\\times 2.4\\,\\text{m}\\times 2.4\\,\\text{m},$  where each dimension is measured to the nearest $0.1\\,\\text{m}.$ Rounded to the nearest tenth of a cubic meter, what is the smallest possible volume of the tank?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186539011 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "cc1f868e-acd2-53b5-a61f-6b065116ee9a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186539011 #edb/ref 1074 "Each dimension is given to the nearest $0.1\\,\\text{m}.$ So, the greatest possible error of these measurements is\n\n$$\n\\frac{0.1\\,\\text{m}}{2}=0.05\\,\\text{m}.\n$$\n\nWe wish to find the *smallest* possible volume of the tank. Therefore, we require the *lower bound* of each dimension.\n\nThe lower bounds for each dimension are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & 2.4\\,\\text{m}-0.05\\,\\text{m}=2.35\\,\\text{m} \\\\  & 2.4\\,\\text{m}-0.05\\,\\text{m}=2.35\\,\\text{m} \\\\  & 2.4\\,\\text{m}-0.05\\,\\text{m}=2.35\\,\\text{m}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the smallest possible volume of the tank is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =2.35\\,\\text{m}\\times 2.35\\,\\text{m}\\times 2.35\\,\\text{m} \\\\  & =12.977\\,875\\,{\\text{m}}^{3} \\\\  & ≈13.0\\,{\\text{m}}^{3},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest tenth of a cubic meter." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186539064 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186539088 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186539064 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "59367d0f-feb4-57b0-bee3-c4cdea97ec6b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186539064 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186539011 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186539066 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "67e2fe74-1f9c-5f39-b367-3beb48c1832e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186539066 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186138737 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186539088 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186539066 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186539088 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "afdc7c1a-9e06-55c0-8af8-0603e546f8f9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186539088 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186138698 #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
